            <ArticleTitle>Iterative reconstruction in cardiac CT.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>255-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcct.2015.04.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-5925(15)00137-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Iterative reconstruction (IR) has the ability to reduce image noise in CT without compromising diagnostic quality, which permits a significant reduction in effective radiation dose. This been increasingly integrated into clinical CT practice over the past 7 years and has been particularly important in the field of cardiac CT with multiple vendors introducing cardiac CT-compatible IR algorithms. The following review will summarize the principles of IR algorithms, studies validating their noise- and dose-reducing abilities, and the specific applications of IR in cardiac CT.</AbstractText>
